  Stupid Pet Trick: Never lose pets to starvation.

  Note: In the following:

    - dog refers to:
      - small dog
      -       dog
      - large dog
    - cat refers to:
      - kitten
      - cat
      - large cat
    - horse refers to:
      - pony
      - horse
      - warhorse


  Situation: Your dog/cat/horse is confused from hunger, and all the
food you have is:

   - one food ration:        800 nutrition
   - one apple:               50 nutrition
   - one fortune cookie:      40 nutrition
   - one tin             unknown nutrition


  40 or 50 nutrition won't help the pet that much.  You want the food
ration for yourself.  (You're saving praying for an emergency.)  You can
avoid the pet until it dies of starvation, but there goes your pet.  You
can hope for a miraculously appropriate corpse.

  Or you can leave the level, leaving the pet behind.  Then wait until
the pet would have starved to death (100 turns?  200 turns?).  

  When you return, the pet will be alive, but hostile.  Hostile monsters
don't seem to have a food status.  

  You can now immediately re-tame it with the appropriate means.  If you
don't have appropriate means (other than the food ration) then you can
make it peaceful by throwing the tin at it.  If it's a horse, you can
make it peaceful by throwing the food ration at it.  Then get your food
ration back.


  This may make the knight's starting inventory of apples, etc. much
more useful for keeping a pet alive.



  Note: A tripe ration can be used instead of a fortune cookie for
dogs/cats.  Just remember that tripe rations have other, possibly
better, uses in conjunctionn with a pet dog/cat.



  This looks like it should work, but I have not tested it.  In the
given situation it surely seems worth a try.  You shouldn't be any worse
off than before.

  Even if this doesn't work, you can still leave the hostile pet on
another level while you search for food elsewhere.  So knights should
never lose their starting steed permanently to starvation.  Although
they may have to do without their horse for awhile.




